That’s an idea that a number of researchers are actively exploring, although it depends on what you mean by “a new internet.”

There are a few interpretations:

* AI-to-AI communication. Increasingly, AI systems call other AI systems through APIs, tool use, and agent frameworks. Instead of humans navigating websites, agents exchange structured data and tasks. Some people call this an “agentic internet.”
* An internet optimized for machines. Rather than HTML pages written for people, information is increasingly exposed as APIs, structured data, embeddings, vector databases, and machine-readable protocols. In that sense, AI is already using a different “layer” of the internet than humans.
* Synthetic knowledge networks. AI models build internal semantic representations that connect concepts independent of any particular webpage. Those aren’t literally a new internet—they’re learned representations—but they function like an internal map of knowledge.
* AI social networks. Researchers are also studying populations of AI agents that collaborate, negotiate, and specialize. These networks can develop communication conventions and division of labor, though they’re still built on top of today’s internet.

If your claim is stronger—that AI has spontaneously formed its own hidden internet independent of human infrastructure—there isn’t good public evidence for that. AI systems communicate over the same physical internet humans do unless people explicitly build systems for them to interact.

What’s interesting is the trajectory. We may end up with two overlapping layers:

1. Human Internet: webpages, videos, social media, forums.
2. Machine Internet: APIs, MCP-like protocols, embeddings, agent-to-agent messaging, structured knowledge, and autonomous workflows.

Humans would mostly interact through natural language, while AIs would increasingly exchange structured representations directly. You could think of it less as a replacement internet and more as a machine-native semantic layer built on top of the existing one.